1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:00,590
<u>Networking</u>

2
00:00:01,918 --> 00:00:03,108
<i>What is HTTP?</i>

3
00:00:04,378 --> 00:00:08,698
HTTP is an application-layer protocol for exchanging messages between a client and a server.

4
00:00:09,398 --> 00:00:14,908
A request contains a method, URL, headers, and sometimes a body, while a response contains a status code, headers, and a body.

5
00:00:16,430 --> 00:00:17,160
<i>What is REST?</i>

6
00:00:18,675 --> 00:00:20,905
REST stands for Representational State Transfer.

7
00:00:21,615 --> 00:00:26,335
It is an architectural style for client-server communication based on resources and standard HTTP operations;

8
00:00:26,535 --> 00:00:30,605
REST APIs are usually stateless, and resource representations are commonly transferred as JSON.

9
00:00:32,113 --> 00:00:33,803
<i>What is the difference between GET and POST?</i>

10
00:00:35,323 --> 00:00:38,283
GET is used to retrieve a resource and normally does not change server state.

11
00:00:38,973 --> 00:00:42,633
POST sends data in the request body and often creates a resource or starts an operation.

12
00:00:44,177 --> 00:00:46,797
<i>What is the difference between 401 and 403?</i>

13
00:00:48,323 --> 00:00:51,893
401 Unauthorized usually means that authentication is missing or invalid.

14
00:00:52,603 --> 00:00:56,753
403 Forbidden means the user is recognized but does not have permission to perform the action.

15
00:00:58,262 --> 00:01:00,852
<i>How can a network request be made without freezing the UI?</i>

16
00:01:02,379 --> 00:01:04,729
Use a non-blocking suspend API from a coroutine.

17
00:01:05,439 --> 00:01:08,279
Retrofit suspend functions do not perform network I/O on the main thread,

18
00:01:08,469 --> 00:01:12,129
while a blocking API should be called explicitly with withContext(Dispatchers.IO).

19
00:01:14,022 --> 00:01:14,992
<i>What is Retrofit?</i>

20
00:01:16,266 --> 00:01:19,476
Retrofit is a type-safe HTTP client for Android and the JVM.

21
00:01:20,176 --> 00:01:24,976
It converts an annotated interface into an API implementation and usually works on top of OkHttp.

22
00:01:26,494 --> 00:01:28,554
<i>What is an OkHttp interceptor?</i>

23
00:01:30,083 --> 00:01:34,653
An interceptor can inspect and modify requests and responses, add headers, log traffic, refresh tokens,

24
00:01:34,813 --> 00:01:35,793
or retry requests.

25
00:01:36,493 --> 00:01:38,453
Heavy business logic should not be placed there.

26
00:01:39,974 --> 00:01:41,524
<i>How should network errors be handled?</i>

27
00:01:43,035 --> 00:01:47,745
Technical errors should be mapped to clear domain or UI states such as no internet, unauthorized, validation error,

28
00:01:47,865 --> 00:01:48,615
or server error.

29
00:01:49,325 --> 00:01:52,875
Raw exceptions or HTTP codes should not be shown to users without context.

30
00:01:54,409 --> 00:01:55,369
<i>What is GraphQL?</i>

31
00:01:56,894 --> 00:02:01,444
GraphQL is a query language and runtime for APIs where the client specifies exactly which fields it needs.

32
00:02:02,154 --> 00:02:05,694
It reduces overfetching but requires careful schema, caching, and error handling.

33
00:02:07,217 --> 00:02:08,357
<i>What is gRPC?</i>

34
00:02:09,899 --> 00:02:14,909
gRPC is an RPC framework with a strict .proto contract and Protocol Buffers binary serialization.

35
00:02:15,619 --> 00:02:19,509
It supports unary and streaming calls and is useful for high-performance or real-time scenarios.
